WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.500
A recovery time objective, or RTO,

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:08.500
is a target for how quickly a system or process should be restored after disruption.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.900
A recovery point objective, or RPO,

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:14.900
describes how much data or configuration change can be lost.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.300
Maximum tolerable downtime marks the point beyond which impact becomes unacceptable.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.300
These objectives need OT interpretation.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:26.100
Restoring an HMI server in two hours does not mean the process

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.500
can safely restart in two hours.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.300
Equipment may require inspection, utilities may need stabilization, logic may need validation,

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.900
and upstream or downstream operations may not be ready.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.300
Safety-first decision-making sets boundaries around speed.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.500
A process should remain shut down or degraded when alarms, interlocks, field states,

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.300
communications, or operator visibility cannot be trusted.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.500
Business pressure does not change those physical conditions.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.500
Prioritize restoration by process dependency.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.900
Foundational utilities, networks, time, identity, controllers,

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.300
and operator visibility may need to recover before historians or reporting.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:04.100
The exact sequence varies by facility and should be engineered rather than

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.100
copied from a generic checklist.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.100
Objectives should include people, spares, vendor response, and startup duration,

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.100
not just backup restore time.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.100
Define who can authorize degraded operation, safe shutdown, technical recovery,

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.700
and return to production.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.100
Escalation paths should resolve conflicts between safety, operations, and business needs.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.300
Make objectives testable.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:28.100
Exercises should measure actual restore and restart times, data loss, validation effort,

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.300
and unmet prerequisites.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.100
If results do not meet the target,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:37.300
improve resources or revise the objective transparently rather than preserving an unrealistic promise.
